    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7bfdb114-e99c-5308-882b-a06ecfe7ec94",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3\\cos(3x) \\\\ &=\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ &\\not\\equiv \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}$ and $y\\prime=\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} &=\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
