    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "96f19602-5c66-5d3a-82c8-dec7f8563edd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=- 5 y\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=5 e^{- 2 x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=- 10 e^{- 2 x}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=5 e^{- 2 x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(5 e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=5\\cdot (-2)\\,e^{- 2 x} \\\\ &=- 10 e^{- 2 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=5 e^{- 2 x}$ and $y\\prime=- 10 e^{- 2 x}$ into the original equation gives expressions that are not identical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}- 5 y \\\\ - 10 e^{- 2 x} &\\overset{?}{=}-5\\left(5 e^{- 2 x}\\right) \\\\ - 10 e^{- 2 x} &\\not\\equiv - 25 e^{- 2 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=5 e^{- 2 x}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "0278840f-5cc0-503d-841f-e52665da3305",
