      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=8 x e^{- \\frac{y}{4}},\\qquad x>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=8 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{8}{x}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=8 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(8 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=8\\cdot\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ &=\\frac{8}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=8 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{8}{x}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}8 x e^{- \\frac{y}{4}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{x} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{8x}{e^{(8 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)})/4}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{x} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{8x}{e^{2\\ln x}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{x} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{8x}{e^{\\ln(x^2)}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{x} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{8x}{x^2} \\\\ \\frac{8}{x} &=\\frac{8}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=8 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
