    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "64b2652d-07ab-56db-a672-1aeaa482cb67",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=5 e^{- y},\\qquad 4 x + 4>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{4}{4 x + 4}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(\\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4 x + 4}\\cdot 4 \\\\ &=\\frac{4}{4 x + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{4}{4 x + 4}$ into the original equation gives expressions that are not identical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}5 e^{- y} \\\\ \\frac{4}{4 x + 4} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5}{e^{\\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)}}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{4 x + 4} &\\not\\equiv \\frac{5}{4 \\left(x + 1\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "b9f1f386-136e-5fa5-8f80-4c4c191ef81c",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
