      ":question/id": "21183b40-a43d-52c8-bcd5-5c3f04c3af54",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=5 e^{- y},\\qquad 5 x + 1>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(5 x + 1 \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{5}{5 x + 1}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(5 x + 1 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(\\ln{\\left(5 x + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{5 x + 1}\\cdot 5 \\\\ &=\\frac{5}{5 x + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\ln{\\left(5 x + 1 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{5}{5 x + 1}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}5 e^{- y} \\\\ \\frac{5}{5 x + 1} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5}{e^{\\ln{\\left(5 x + 1 \\right)}}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{5 x + 1} &=\\frac{5}{5 x + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\ln{\\left(5 x + 1 \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
